1. Introduction

Technological civilization can be understood as a sequence of transformations in which new capacities for processing energy, matter, information, and organization are coupled into stable socio-technical regimes.

Let Z_t denote the state of a civilization at time t. We define the following transition sequence:

\text{Energy} \rightarrow \text{Mechanical Work}

\text{Information} \rightarrow \text{Computation}

\text{Information} \rightarrow \text{Machine Reasoning}

The emerging transition is:

\text{Machine Reasoning} \rightarrow \text{Autonomous Action}

A conventional AI system answers a question. An agentic system interprets an objective, decomposes it into sub-objectives, acquires information, selects tools, executes actions, evaluates outcomes, revises its strategy, coordinates with other agents, and persists under changing conditions. This transition is qualitatively different because it converts computation into persistent, world-changing action.

We define an agentic civilization as:

A civilization in which autonomous or semi-autonomous computational agents become persistent participants in the production, coordination, maintenance, discovery, and governance of social and physical systems.

The central question is not:

How intelligent can an AI become?

But:

How much civilization can a society safely build with increasingly autonomous intelligence?

And, beyond Earth:

What minimum agentic civilization is required for humanity to become genuinely multiplanetary?

This manuscript builds a formal theory to address these questions.

