5. Energy, Compute, and Manufacturing as Vector Constraints

5.1 Energy Constraint

The maximum sustainable agentic capability is bounded above by the energy system state:

C_A(Z) \le f(E_{\text{gen},t}, E_{\text{grid},t}, E_{\text{cool},t}, E_{\text{fuel},t})

We define the energy sufficiency ratio:

r_E = \min\left( \frac{E_{\text{gen}}}{E_{\text{gen}}^{\text{req}}}, \frac{E_{\text{grid}}}{E_{\text{grid}}^{\text{req}}}, \frac{E_{\text{cool}}}{E_{\text{cool}}^{\text{req}}}, \frac{E_{\text{fuel}}}{E_{\text{fuel}}^{\text{req}}} \right)

Then:

C_A(Z) \le \Phi_E(r_E)

where \Phi_E is monotone.

5.2 Compute Constraint

Intelligence growth is not autonomous. It requires compute and data:

I_{t+1} = I_t + g_I(S_t, C_{\text{hw},t}, C_{\text{data},t}, O_t) - \delta_I I_t

Compute hardware is constrained by advanced packaging, memory supply, and energy. Data is constrained by finite high-quality sources and may approach exhaustion. Thus:

\frac{\partial I}{\partial C_{\text{hw}}} > 0, \quad \frac{\partial I}{\partial C_{\text{data}}} > 0, \quad \frac{\partial I}{\partial O_t} \ge 0

5.3 Partial Industrial Closure

Full self-replication is not a binary threshold. We define partial industrial closure metrics:

\phi_{\text{mass}} = \frac{\text{robot mass producible locally}}{\text{total robot mass required}}

\phi_{\text{maintenance}} = \frac{\text{maintenance actions automatable}}{\text{total maintenance actions required}}

\phi_{\text{energy}} = \frac{\text{energy generated locally}}{\text{energy required}}

\phi_{\text{control}} = \frac{\text{control decisions made without Earth oversight}}{\text{total control decisions required}}

The aggregate closure index is:

\Phi_A = \min_j \phi_j

Proposition 3 (Closure Necessary Condition).
For a physical system to maintain itself indefinitely without external intervention, \Phi_A = 1. For partial autonomy, \Phi_A must exceed a critical fraction \Phi_{\min} determined by external replenishment capacity.

Proof sketch. If any closure fraction is below 1, the corresponding deficit must be met externally. A sustainable steady state requires external inputs to match deficits. If external inputs are insufficient, the deficit accumulates and the system fails. ∎

This maps directly to terrestrial supply-chain resilience and to off-world settlement.

