6. Multi-Agent Coordination and Safety Verification

6.1 Coordination Transparency

Multi-agent interaction is not merely a capability; it is a risk vector. Unconstrained populations of agents can collude, conflict, and produce emergent failure modes.

We define coordination transparency as a governance requirement: oversight must target interaction patterns and protocols, not just individual agent outputs.

Formally:

G_{\text{coord},t} = f(\text{protocol observability}, \text{collusion detection}, \text{circuit breaker availability})

Let n_t be the number of interacting agents. We model collusion probability as:

P(\text{collusion}_t) = f(A_t, n_t, O_{\text{monitor},t}, O_{\text{verify},t})

with partial derivatives:

\frac{\partial P}{\partial A_t} > 0, \quad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial n_t} > 0, \quad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial O_{\text{monitor},t}} < 0, \quad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial O_{\text{verify},t}} < 0

Lemma 1 (Verification Reduces Collusion).
For fixed autonomy A_t and agent count n_t, an increase in monitoring and verification capability strictly reduces the probability of severe collusion.

Proof sketch. By assumption, P is strictly decreasing in O_monitor and O_verify. ∎

This lemma formalizes the empirical observation that institutional governance graphs can reduce severe collusion from high-frequency to low-frequency regimes.

6.2 Multi-Agent Verification

We adopt the concept of Multi-Agent Verification (MAV). A compositional assume-guarantee contract framework allows verification of global safety properties by checking local agent contracts.

Let \mathcal{C}_i be a contract for agent i, specifying assumptions A_i and guarantees G_i. The global property \mathcal{P} holds if:

\bigwedge_i \mathcal{C}_i \Rightarrow \mathcal{P}

under compatibility conditions on shared variables.

This provides a formal path from individual agent safety to systemic safety.

