7. Human Agency and the Viability Kernel

7.1 Operationalizing Human Agency

Human agency is not a scalar. We operationalize it through three measurable conditions:

p_{\text{override}} \ge p_{\min}

\tau_{\text{comprehension}} \le \tau_{\max}

v_{\text{critical}} \ge v_{\min}

where:

· p_override: probability that humans can successfully override an agentic system
· τ_comprehension: time required for humans to understand a system's action
· v_critical: fraction of critical decisions with meaningful human veto

These conditions are difficult to measure but are falsifiable in principle.

7.2 Acceptable State Set

Define the set of acceptable states:

\mathcal{V} = \{ Z \in \mathcal{Z} : E(Z) > E_{\min}, M(Z) > M_{\min}, H(Z) \ge H_{\min}, G(Z) \ge G_{\min}, O(Z) \ge O_{\min}, R(Z) > R_{\min} \}

7.3 Probabilistic Viability Kernel

Because the dynamics are stochastic, viability must be defined probabilistically.

Let \pi be a policy governing the allocation of resources, autonomy, and oversight. Define the probabilistic viability kernel:

\mathcal{K}_\varepsilon = \{ Z_0 : \exists \pi \text{ such that } P( Z_t \in \mathcal{V} \; \forall t \ge 0 ) \ge 1 - \varepsilon \}

where \varepsilon is the accepted failure probability.

Proposition 4 (Viability Kernel Properties).
Under Lipschitz dynamics and compact \mathcal{V}, the viability kernel \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon is closed and is the largest control-invariant subset of \mathcal{V}.

Proof sketch. Standard viability theory: the kernel is the set of initial states for which there exists a control policy keeping trajectories in \mathcal{V} with probability at least 1 - \varepsilon. Closedness follows from continuity of trajectories. ∎

7.4 Computational Approximation

Exact computation of high-dimensional viability kernels is intractable. We propose approximation via:

· neural viability networks
· set-valued machine learning
· Monte Carlo trajectory ensembles
· Koopman operator methods for reduced-order dynamics
· dimensional reduction into (D_t, P_t, K_t, O_t)

