8. Multiplanetary Extension

8.1 Robot-First Settlement

Mars–Earth round-trip communication latency is 8–40 minutes. This makes real-time human oversight impossible for time-critical operations.

We define a robot-first settlement framework: a preparatory phase using autonomous robots before human arrival, emphasizing failure-learning cycles, local resource utilization, and autonomous maintenance.

8.2 Off-World Closure Requirement

A viable settlement requires:

\text{Local AI} + \text{Robotics} + \text{Energy} + \text{Manufacturing} + \text{Food} + \text{Maintenance} + \text{Scientific Discovery}

The partial closure metrics become critical:

\Phi_A^{\text{Mars}} = \min(\phi_{\text{mass}}, \phi_{\text{maintenance}}, \phi_{\text{energy}}, \phi_{\text{control}})

A settlement is not viable until all critical closure fractions exceed settlement-specific thresholds.

Proposition 5 (Latency-Driven Autonomy Threshold).
If round-trip communication latency \tau_L exceeds the required response time for a fraction f of settlement operations, then the off-world control closure fraction must satisfy:

\phi_{\text{control}} \ge f

Proof sketch. Operations with required response time less than \tau_L cannot be remotely supervised. Therefore they must be locally autonomous, contributing directly to the numerator of \phi_{\text{control}}. ∎

8.3 Agentic Settlement Is Not Viable Settlement

Agentic infrastructure is necessary but not sufficient. A viable settlement also requires closed-loop biological life support, food production, medical capacity, social reproduction, and political stability.

Therefore:

\text{Agentic Settlement} \ne \text{Viable Settlement}

