9. Transition Indicators and Threshold Derivation

We define transition indicators as continuous functions of the state vector. Thresholds are not arbitrary; they are derived from viability constraints or empirical analogues.

Trajectory Indicator Threshold Derivation
A: Human-Augmented Civilization Human decision-rights ratio r_H r_H \ge \rho_H where \rho_H is the minimum fraction of tasks for which p_override \ge p_min and τ_comprehension \le τ_max.
B: Agentic Civilization Agentic GDP share s_A s_A \ge \sigma_A where \sigma_A is the share at which agentic production affects aggregate factor demand.
C: Concentrated Machine Economy Ownership concentration H_5 Top-5 ownership exceeds \theta derived from market failure risk models.
D: Systemic Failure Correlated failures per year N_f N_f \ge 3 based on historical critical infrastructure failure tolerance.
E: Multiplanetary Agentic Civilization Off-world autonomy index \Phi_{\text{Mars}} \Phi_{\text{Mars}} \ge 0.9 from latency-driven autonomy threshold.

For any threshold \theta, we define a sensitivity function:

T_\theta(t) = \text{classification time under threshold } \theta

and report \partial T_\theta / \partial \theta to quantify robustness.

