10. Hypotheses and Predictions

We propose twelve hypotheses, each expressible as a prediction of the formal model.

· H1 – Agency Threshold: Autonomous economic value increases nonlinearly once agents achieve reliable long-horizon planning.
· H2 – Physical Bottleneck: After digital reasoning becomes sufficiently inexpensive, physical infrastructure becomes the dominant constraint.
· H3 – Robotics Coupling: Economic value of AI agents increases superlinearly when coupled with general-purpose robotics.
· H4 – Energy Coupling: Maximum sustainable agentic capability is bounded by energy generation, transmission, cooling, and fuel.
· H5 – Partial Closure Acceleration: Even partial industrial closure significantly accelerates autonomous expansion.
· H6 – Multi-Agent Governance Constraint: Unconstrained multi-agent interaction increases systemic fragility even when individual alignment is acceptable.
· H7 – Multiplanetary Necessity: Long-term multiplanetary civilization requires autonomous local agentic infrastructure.
· H8 – Coordination Transparency: Interaction-level governance reduces collusion and cascading failure risk compared to individual-level oversight alone.
· H9 – Human Agency Compatibility: Human agency can be maintained while machine autonomy increases if oversight targets critical decisions, comprehension time, and override probability.
· H10 – Governance Gap: The limiting factor for safe agentic civilization is institutional adaptation speed.
· H11 – Race Condition: Competitive pressure between geopolitical blocs increases fragmentation and degrades governance, accelerating intelligence while degrading safety.
· H12 – Data Wall: As high-quality data approaches exhaustion, intelligence growth slows unless compute efficiency or synthetic data quality improves sufficiently.

Each hypothesis maps to specific terms in the dynamical equations, enabling simulation-based testing.

