11. Simulation Architecture and Implementation Roadmap

No simulations or physical tests have been conducted. This section provides a technical specification for future implementation.

11.1 Numerical Methods

· Stochastic delay differential equations: Euler–Maruyama with fixed lag interpolation.
· Viability kernel approximation: Neural viability networks trained on Monte Carlo trajectory ensembles.
· Sensitivity analysis: Automatic differentiation through the simulation graph.
· Dimensional reduction: Koopman operator methods applied to the grouped system.

11.2 Calibration Data Sources

· International Energy Agency (IEA) data center electricity demand
· U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) grid interconnection and generation data
· Semiconductor production and advanced packaging statistics
· Robotics benchmark performance
· Enterprise AI adoption surveys
· Multi-agent safety benchmarks
· Historical infrastructure buildout timescales

11.3 Validation Strategy

Compare simulated bottleneck dynamics with observed data-center buildout delays, grid interconnection queues, and manufacturing lead times. Validate viability kernel boundaries against historical critical infrastructure failure cases.

11.4 Implementation Stages

1. Stage 0: Implement the grouped four-variable model (D_t, P_t, K_t, O_t).
2. Stage 1: Add compute–data vector and energy sub-vector.
3. Stage 2: Introduce stochastic volatility and delay terms.
4. Stage 3: Approximate viability kernel using neural networks.
5. Stage 4: Calibrate against empirical time series and run scenario analysis.

