12. Conclusion

The next phase of artificial intelligence will not be defined by the arrival of a single model that crosses an arbitrary intelligence threshold. It will be defined by the coupling of many capabilities:

\text{Intelligence} + \text{Agency} + \text{Robotics} + \text{Energy} + \text{Manufacturing} + \text{Infrastructure} + \text{Science} + \text{Governance} + \text{Safety} + \text{Compute} + \text{Data}

under the constraint of geopolitical fragmentation.

Intelligence becomes civilizationally transformative only when it can persistently convert goals into coordinated action across digital and physical environments under constraints of energy, resources, uncertainty, coordination, safety, and institutional legitimacy.

The central research question remains:

How much civilization can a society safely build with increasingly autonomous intelligence?

And beyond Earth:

What minimum agentic civilization is required for humanity to become genuinely multiplanetary?

This manuscript provides a formal foundation for answering those questions through coupled dynamical systems, viability theory, task-portfolio capability measures, partial industrial closure metrics, and simulation-based falsification.

