Appendix B. Proofs of Main Results

B.1 Proposition 1 — Bottleneck Dominance

Statement.
Assume that for every civilization-critical task τ, the reliability function satisfies:

q(\tau, Z) \le \phi\left( \min_{i} r_i(Z) \right)

where \phi: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to [0,1] is monotone nondecreasing. Suppose also that every task in T requires positive levels of all four grouped capabilities D_t, P_t, K_t, O_t, meaning that if any grouped capability is zero, then q(\tau, Z) = 0 for all τ. Then:

1. For all Z,

C_A(Z) \le \mu\left( \{ \tau : \phi(\min_i r_i(Z)) \ge p(\tau) \} \right)

2. C_A(Z) > 0 implies:

\min(D_t, P_t, K_t, O_t) > 0

Proof.

(1) For any τ such that q(τ,Z) ≥ p(τ), the upper bound implies:

\phi(\min_i r_i(Z)) \ge q(\tau, Z) \ge p(\tau)

Therefore:

\{ \tau : q(\tau, Z) \ge p(\tau) \} \subseteq \{ \tau : \phi(\min_i r_i(Z)) \ge p(\tau) \}

Integrating over T with weight w gives the claimed inequality.

(2) Suppose C_A(Z) > 0. Then there exists a set of positive measure on which q(τ,Z) ≥ p(τ). By the assumption that every task requires all four grouped capabilities, if any of D_t, P_t, K_t, O_t were zero, then q(τ,Z) = 0 for all τ, making C_A(Z) = 0. Since C_A(Z) > 0, all four grouped capabilities must be strictly positive. ∎

---

B.2 Proposition 2 — Timescale-Mismatch Instability

Statement.
Let D_t and P_t grow exponentially:

D_t = D_0 e^{r_D t}, \qquad P_t = P_0 e^{r_P t}

with r_D > r_P > 0. Define physical slack:

S_P(t) = P_t - \kappa_P D_t

where \kappa_P > 0 is the digital-to-physical demand coefficient. Then there exists t^* < \infty such that:

\frac{dS_P}{dt} < 0 \quad \forall t \ge t^*

and S_P(t) < 0 for all sufficiently large t.

Proof.

Differentiate:

\frac{dS_P}{dt} = \frac{dP_t}{dt} - \kappa_P \frac{dD_t}{dt}

= r_P P_0 e^{r_P t} - \kappa_P r_D D_0 e^{r_D t}

= r_P P_0 e^{r_P t} \left( 1 - \frac{\kappa_P r_D D_0}{r_P P_0} e^{(r_D - r_P)t} \right)

Since r_D - r_P > 0, the exponential term grows without bound. Hence there exists a unique t^* such that:

\frac{\kappa_P r_D D_0}{r_P P_0} e^{(r_D - r_P)t^*} = 1

For all t > t^*, the bracket is negative, so dS_P/dt < 0.

Integrating, S_P(t) becomes negative when:

P_0 e^{r_P t} < \kappa_P D_0 e^{r_D t}

which holds for all sufficiently large t because r_D > r_P. ∎

---

B.3 Proposition 3 — Closure Necessary Condition

Statement.
For a physical system to maintain itself indefinitely without external intervention, the aggregate closure index must satisfy:

\Phi_A = \min_j \phi_j = 1

If any closure fraction \phi_j < 1, then indefinite operation requires external replenishment. If external replenishment is bounded, the system fails when accumulated deficits exceed its buffer capacity.

Proof.

Let R_j(t) be the required flow of resource j and L_j(t) the locally produced flow. Define closure fraction:

\phi_j = \frac{L_j}{R_j}

The deficit flow is:

D_j(t) = R_j(t) - L_j(t) = R_j(t)(1 - \phi_j)

If \phi_j = 1, then D_j = 0 and no external input is needed.

If \phi_j < 1, then D_j > 0. Let B_j(t) be the buffer stock of resource j. The buffer evolves as:

\frac{dB_j}{dt} = -D_j(t) + E_j(t)

where E_j(t) is external replenishment. If E_j(t) < D_j(t) on average, then B_j decreases. If E_j(t) is bounded above by E_j^{\max} and D_j(t) > E_j^{\max} persistently, then:

B_j(t) \to -\infty

which is physically impossible; the system fails when B_j reaches zero. Thus \Phi_A = 1 is necessary for indefinite self-maintenance without external input. ∎

---

B.4 Lemma 1 — Verification Reduces Collusion

Statement.
Let collusion probability be:

P_{\text{coll}} = f(A_t, n_t, O_{\text{monitor},t}, O_{\text{verify},t})

with f differentiable and:

\frac{\partial f}{\partial O_{\text{monitor}}} < 0, \qquad \frac{\partial f}{\partial O_{\text{verify}}} < 0

Then for fixed A_t and n_t, increasing monitoring or verification strictly reduces P_{\text{coll}}.

Proof.

Immediate from the sign assumptions. ∎

---

B.5 Proposition 4 — Viability Kernel Properties

Statement.
Consider the stochastic differential inclusion:

dZ_t \in F(Z_t, u_t) dt + \Sigma(Z_t) dW_t

with u_t ∈ U(Z_t), compact control set, Lipschitz F and Σ, and a closed acceptable set \mathcal{V}. Define:

\mathcal{K}_\varepsilon = \{ Z_0 : \exists \pi \text{ such that } P( Z_t \in \mathcal{V} \ \forall t \ge 0 ) \ge 1 - \varepsilon \}

Then \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon is closed and is the largest control-invariant subset of \mathcal{V}.

Proof.

We sketch the standard viability argument.

Closedness. Let Z_0^n \in \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon with Z_0^n \to Z_0. For each n, there exists a policy \pi^n such that the probability of staying in \mathcal{V} is at least 1 - \varepsilon. By compactness of control sets and Lipschitz continuity of trajectories with respect to initial data, extract a subsequence of policies and trajectories converging to a limit trajectory starting at Z_0. The limit trajectory remains in \mathcal{V} with probability at least 1 - \varepsilon by the portmanteau theorem. Hence Z_0 \in \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon.

Largest control-invariant subset. Any point in \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon has a policy ensuring viability. Conversely, if Z_0 \notin \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon, then for every policy, the probability of exiting \mathcal{V} exceeds \varepsilon, so no policy can make Z_0 viable. Thus \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon contains exactly the points that can be made viable by some policy; it is the largest such set. ∎

---

B.6 Proposition 5 — Latency-Driven Autonomy Threshold

Statement.
Let \tau_L be the round-trip communication latency between a settlement and its control center. Let each operation j have a required response time \tau_{\text{req},j}. Define:

f = \frac{\mu( \{ j : \tau_{\text{req},j} < \tau_L \} )}{\mu(\text{all operations})}

Then the control closure fraction must satisfy:

\phi_{\text{control}} \ge f

Proof.

Operations with \tau_{\text{req},j} < \tau_L cannot be remotely supervised, because the minimum round-trip delay exceeds the required response time. Therefore all such operations must be executed by local autonomous decision-making. The fraction of decisions that must be local is at least f. By definition, \phi_{\text{control}} is the fraction of control decisions made without external oversight. Hence:

\phi_{\text{control}} \ge f

∎

