Appendix C. Task-Portfolio Capability: Formal Properties and Approximation Bounds

C.1 Monotonicity and Continuity

Proposition C.1.
If q(τ, Z) is nondecreasing in each component of Z for μ-a.e. τ, then C_A(Z) is nondecreasing in each component.

Proof. If Z_1 \le Z_2 componentwise, then q(τ,Z_1) \le q(τ,Z_2), so the set of tasks satisfying q \ge p is larger under Z_2. Integration gives C_A(Z_1) \le C_A(Z_2). ∎

Proposition C.2.
If Z \mapsto q(τ, Z) is continuous for μ-a.e. τ, then C_A(Z) is continuous.

Proof. Since q is bounded and continuous a.e., the indicator function \mathbf{1}\{ q(τ,Z) \ge p(τ) \} is continuous except possibly on the boundary set \{ q(τ,Z) = p(τ) \}. If the measure of this boundary set is zero, dominated convergence gives continuity. If boundary sets have positive measure, the function may have discontinuities; however, under a generic regularity assumption, the boundary set has μ-measure zero, yielding continuity. ∎

C.2 Smooth Bottleneck Approximation Bound

Proposition C.3.
Let r_i > 0 for all i, and define:

C_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} = \left( \sum_i r_i^{-k} \right)^{-1/k}

Then for all k > 0:

n^{-1/k} \min_i r_i \le C_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} \le \min_i r_i

and:

\lim_{k \to \infty} C_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} = \min_i r_i

Proof.

Let m = \min_i r_i. Since r_i \ge m, we have r_i^{-k} \le m^{-k}, so:

\sum_i r_i^{-k} \le n m^{-k}

Thus:

C_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} \ge n^{-1/k} m

Also, since at least one r_i = m, the sum is at least m^{-k}, so:

C_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} \le m

Taking the limit as k \to \infty, n^{-1/k} \to 1, so the lower bound converges to m, and the upper bound is always m. Hence the limit is m. ∎

C.3 Thresholded Multiplicative Approximation

Proposition C.4.
Define:

C_{\text{mult}} = \prod_i \max\left(0, \frac{X_i - X_i^{\text{crit}}}{X_i^* - X_i^{\text{crit}}}\right)^{\alpha_i}

Then C_{\text{mult}} = 0 if any X_i \le X_i^{\text{crit}}, and C_{\text{mult}} is nondecreasing in each X_i.

Proof. Immediate from the maximum term. ∎

