Appendix D. Coupled Dynamics and Timescale Separation

D.1 Well-Posedness of the Delay-Differential System

Theorem D.1.
Consider:

dZ_t = F(Z_t, Z_{t-\tau}, u_t) dt + \Sigma(Z_t) dW_t

with F globally Lipschitz in both arguments, Σ globally Lipschitz, and τ a fixed vector of delays. Then there exists a unique strong solution for every initial history.

Proof. Standard existence and uniqueness for stochastic delay differential equations under Lipschitz conditions. ∎

D.2 Lyapunov Stability of the Coupled System

Define the grouped system:

\frac{dD_t}{dt} = \alpha_D S_t C_{\text{data},t}^{\beta_D} C_{\text{hw},t}^{\gamma_D} - \delta_D D_t

\frac{dP_t}{dt} = \eta_P P_t \left( 1 - \frac{P_t}{P_{\max}} \right) + \theta_{E,M} E_t M_t - \kappa_P D_t - \delta_P P_t

\frac{dK_t}{dt} = \lambda_K H_t - \mu_F F_t - \nu_A A_t - \delta_K K_t

\frac{dO_t}{dt} = \rho_O K_t + \sigma_O (O_{\text{verify},t} + O_{\text{monitor},t}) - \omega_A A_t - \delta_O O_t

Proposition D.2.
If δ_D, δ_P, δ_K, δ_O > 0 and all source terms are bounded, then the system has a bounded forward invariant region.

Proof. Each equation has a linear dissipative term -δ_i X_i; bounded inputs imply bounded steady states. The quadratic damping in the physical equation prevents blow-up. Standard comparison arguments yield boundedness. ∎

