Appendix E. Viability Kernel Approximation Theory

E.1 Neural Viability Networks

Let N_\theta: \mathcal{Z} \to [0,1] be a neural network with parameters θ. We approximate the viability kernel by:

\mathcal{K}_\varepsilon \approx \{ Z : N_\theta(Z) \ge 0.5 \}

Training objective:

\min_\theta \mathbb{E}_{Z_0 \sim \mu_0} \left[ \left( N_\theta(Z_0) - P(Z_t \in \mathcal{V} \ \forall t) \right)^2 \right]

where the probability is estimated by Monte Carlo simulation under a candidate policy.

E.2 Monte Carlo Convergence

By the law of large numbers, for N independent trajectory samples:

\hat{P}_N = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=1}^N \mathbf{1}\{ Z_t^j \in \mathcal{V} \ \forall t \}

converges almost surely to the true probability. The error is O(N^{-1/2}).

